Confirmed: `router` is an external npm dependency (`router: "^2.2.0"`), not implemented in this repo. Now let's see how application.js wires it in.Let's check the VERB delegation section (methods.forEach) to complete the picture.## Findings

**Application-level route handling is wired up in `lib/application.js`, but the actual router logic is *not* implemented in this repository — it's pulled in from the separate `router` npm package.**

### Where wiring happens

- **`lib/application.js:26`** — `var Router = require('router');` imports the external package.
- **`lib/application.js:59-82`** (`app.init`) — lazily instantiates the app's base router:
  ```js
  Object.defineProperty(this, 'router', {
    get: function getrouter() {
      if (router === null) {
        router = new Router({ caseSensitive: ..., strict: ... });
      }
      return router;
    }
  });
  ```
- **`lib/application.js:177`** (`app.handle`) — every incoming request is dispatched to it: `this.router.handle(req, res, done);`
- **`app.use`** (~line 216-222) proxies straight to `router.use(path, fn)`, with extra logic only for mounting sub-apps (`fn.handle`/`fn.mountpath`/emitting `'mount'`).
- **`app.route`** (line 257) proxies to `this.router.route(path)`.
- **`app.param`** (line 331) proxies to `this.router.param(name, fn)`.
- **`methods.forEach` block (line 471-472)** — dynamically creates `app.get/post/put/...` by delegating to `this.route(path)[method](...)` (VERB methods list comes from `lib/utils.js`'s `methods` export).

### Router is an external dependency, not implemented here

- **`package.json`** dependencies: `"router": "^2.2.0"`.
- **`lib/express.js`** re-exports the package's classes directly:
  ```js
  var Router = require('router');
  ...
  exports.Route = Router.Route;
  exports.Router = Router;
  ```
- I confirmed there is **no `lib/router.js` or `lib/route.js`** in this repo — `ghx tree expressjs/express lib --depth 3` lists only `application.js, express.js, request.js, response.js, utils.js, view.js`. The routing tree, path-matching, and `Route`/`Layer` implementation live in the separate `pillarjs/router` package (https://github.com/pillarjs/router), consumed as a normal npm dependency.

### What I did not verify

- I did not open the actual `router` package source (it's a different repo/npm package, out of scope per the instructions to work only within `expressjs/express`).
- I did not exhaustively read `lib/utils.js` for the full `methods` array contents (only confirmed it's imported and used to generate verb methods).
- I did not check `test/` for behavioral confirmation of the delegation — this is based on static reading of `lib/application.js` and `lib/express.js` only.

